# syscall-ptr.manifest - the syscall-ptr-lint allowlist (#565, enforcing #500).
#
# tools/syscall-ptr-lint/syscall-ptr-lint auto-discovers every SYS_* whose
# dispatcher case in proc/syscall.c casts an argument to a USER POINTER. Each
# such syscall must have EITHER a flat descriptor in rustkern/argtab.rs (the
# common case, 114 syscalls) OR an entry below. A pointer syscall that is in
# neither FAILS THE BUILD. This file is that "OR": it is wired into the build
# as an order-only prerequisite of kernel.elf (make syscall-ptr-lint), so the
# hole #500 closed cannot silently reopen on the next syscall added.
#
# Two sections, and the distinction is the whole point of being honest here:
#
# [EXEMPT]    The handler validates the user pointer ITSELF, so a flat argtab
#             descriptor is the wrong tool (the length is cmd-dependent, or the
#             same handler has legitimate kernel-pointer callers, or it is a
#             debug-only build). These are permanently correct, not debt.
#
# [KNOWN_GAP] A DEBT LEDGER: a real, currently-UNVALIDATED Ring-3 pointer hole
#             that #500 missed and this lint surfaced (#565). It is TRACKED and
#             printed loudly on every build, but tolerated (build not wedged)
#             until a descriptor is added in argtab.rs. This is the opposite of
#             a silent exemption: every entry names the unvalidated arg and the
#             owning follow-up. CLOSE THESE, then delete the line.
#
# First token per line is the SYS_* name (must be a real #define, typo-guarded);
# the rest is the reason. Add a name here only with a real justification, in the
# same change; the CHANGELOG must say why.

[EXEMPT]
SYS_IOCTL           arg3 is BOUNCED through a kernel buffer at the Ring-3 call site in proc/syscall.c:3722-3736: tty_ioctl_user_desc() returns the cmd-dependent size and direction, then copy_from_user / copy_to_user move it, which is #509 atomic check-and-use rather than a U/S test. length is cmd-dependent (termios/winsize/int) and file_ioctl() also has kernel-pointer callers (gui/terminal.c, net/ssh/ssh2_server.c), so a flat descriptor cannot describe it (#500). CORRECTED 2026-10-04 [badexempt]: this row used to name tty_ioctl_validate_user_arg() as the validator. That function is in drivers/tty.c:402, NOT proc/syscall.c, and it has ZERO callers since #509 replaced the validate-then-use pattern with the bounce (docs/TERMINAL_TTY_STATE.md:558 already recorded it as dead). An [EXEMPT] row naming dead code as the reason a user pointer is safe would survive someone deleting the mechanism that actually makes it safe.
SYS_AUDIO_PCM_WRITE arg2 validated in audio_pcm_write() (drivers/audio_pcm.c) by pcm_user_range_ok() over frames*ch*sizeof(int16_t); the byte count depends on the stream's channel count, not a compile-time sizeof (#500).
SYS_FUTEX           arg1/arg5 validated by validate_futex_addr() -> validate_user_ptr(ACCESS_RW_USER) on EVERY futex path (sync/futex.c: futex_wait/wake/requeue/cmp_requeue) before any *addr deref (#430).
SYS_SECTEST         DEBUG-ONLY negative control, compiled only under -DSECTEST_SYSCALL and never shipped; it MUST receive its pointer untouched to build the boundary cases the validator self-test rejects (see the case comment in proc/syscall.c). No descriptor by design.

[KNOWN_GAP]
